WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:06.290
So I just want to talk about this image that
Cajal drew, of the retina of the lizard.

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:10.469
Cajal was very excited about the retina, I
think in part because of his connection to

00:00:10.469 --> 00:00:11.849
it as an artist.

00:00:11.849 --> 00:00:16.180
And what's really interesting about this image
is that he's depicted many different types

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:17.710
of retinal ganglion cells.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:22.240
And in particular there are many different
types and we know now that the reason for

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.970
these different types is to do with the function
of these different cells.

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:30.600
At the time, Cajal would not have known what
the functions were because he was not able

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.300
to image live tissue.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:36.160
But he was able to notice that these things
did have different types, and therefore could

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.620
postulate that maybe the functions would be
different.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:43.430
And I think this is a testament to what Cajal
was able to infer with the limited data that

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:48.300
he had, as well as how we are then able to
use that information with new techniques,

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:53.360
and being able to do imaging within live animals
to determine the really interesting and varied

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.780
functions of these different types of cells.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:01.550
So here, you have cells that might correspond
to, for example, seeing a red or green object

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:05.239
in the world, or seeing a moving object or
a still object.

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:09.210
And now we know a lot more about the retina
because of these new functional techniques.

